  "content": {
    "id": "p19-r6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "Let $f(x)=x^2+4$ and $g(x)=x-x^3$. Define the function $h(x)=f(x)g(x)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p19-r6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $h(-x)=-h(x)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$h(-x)=(x^2+4)(-x+x^3)=-h(x)$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}h(-x)&=f(-x)g(-x)\\\\h(-x)&=\\bigl((-x)^2+4\\bigr)\\bigl((-x)-(-x)^3\\bigr)\\\\h(-x)&=(x^2+4)(-x+x^3)\\\\h(-x)&=-(x^2+4)(x-x^3)\\\\h(-x)&=-h(x).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-r6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Note that $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are even and odd functions respectively. What do you hypothesise is true about the product of an even and odd function?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The product of an even function and an odd function is odd.",
          "worked": "Part (a) gives $h(-x)=-h(x)$, the defining identity for an odd function. This suggests that the product of an even function and an odd function is odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p19-r6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Prove your guess from part (b).",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For even $F$ and odd $G$, their product $H=FG$ satisfies $H(-x)=F(x)(-G(x))=-H(x)$, so $H$ is odd.",
          "worked": "Let $F$ be any even function and $G$ any odd function, and define $H(x)=F(x)G(x)$ on their common domain. Then $F(-x)=F(x)$ and $G(-x)=-G(x)$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}H(-x)&=F(-x)G(-x)\\\\H(-x)&=F(x)\\bigl(-G(x)\\bigr)\\\\H(-x)&=-F(x)G(x)\\\\H(-x)&=-H(x).\\end{align*}$$\n\nTherefore the product is odd.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      }
    ]
  },
